%% --
%% TWO VERSION NUMBERS, and conflating them is the easy mistake:
%%   RENDER_VERSION :35 is the string "2" and is INSIDE the source hash - bump it
%%     to invalidate every SVG at once.
%%   MANIFEST_VERSION :40 is the integer 2 and describes the manifest FORMAT -
%%     version 2 entries carry svgHash; a version-1 entry cannot be content-checked.
%% They are both 2 today, which is precisely when a reader stops distinguishing them.
%% --
%% The sentence this diagram was drawn to make visible, and what it bought:
%%   Until manifest version 2, --check verified the INPUTS, not the output. `current`
%%   was  have["hash"] == want and svg.exists()  :390 - the hash covering
%%   RENDER_VERSION, the config fingerprint, the background colour and the normalised
%%   source, and nothing at all about the SVG's CONTENT. A committed SVG that had been
%%   truncated, hand-edited or corrupted after rendering passed --check as long as the
%%   file was still there and the source had not moved. That was this repository's
%%   recurring failure in its CI costume: a green check reporting on the thing it could
%%   measure cheaply, wearing the name of the thing you wanted verified.
%%   MEASURED before the fix rather than argued: a real 15902-byte mmdc render cut in
%%   half mid-attribute, and a zero-byte file, BOTH printed "All 1 diagram(s) up to
%%   date." and exited 0.
%% Now: render records svgHash :424 and --check compares it :403. A manifest predating
%%   version 2 has no hash to compare, so it gets a structural check :407 and is
%%   reported UNVERIFIED rather than counted as up to date.
%% UNVERIFIED exited 0 until 1.2.2, on the argument that structurally-intact is the
%%   strongest claim available without a recorded hash. It is - and that is the reason
%%   to go red, not a reason to stay green: the caveat was in the output while the exit
%%   code, which is the only part CI reads, still said pass. A green line meaning less
%%   than its reader assumes is the defect this diagram was drawn to expose, so the
%%   fix kept reproducing it one layer down. It now exits 1 :470, naming render --force
%%   as the one-time fix :466-469, and stays distinct in the text from DAMAGED :461-465.
%%   Both failures are reported in one pass :435-451 - stale, damaged and unverified
%%   sections print together before the single exit - because returning on the first
%%   category found is how the unverified count used to vanish whenever anything else
%%   was wrong. The reasoning is in the code at :430-434.
%%   postprocess()'s foreignObject refusal is still a RENDER-time gate only; it never
%%   runs in CI strategy A, which is the recommended one and needs neither Node nor
%%   Chromium precisely because it does not render.

